speechtext:
Is the Minister of Public Works considering the question of dredging the mouth of the Au Sable river? Ten years ago the government expended about $17,000 in building a wharf at the mouth of that river and since then they have made an added expenditure to the extent of about $25,000. Vessels of a reasonable size are not able to approach the wharf and in summer time the mouth of the river is completely closed. The wharf is of very little use owing to the fact that the aproaches are not owned by the government and people going there are continually being warned off. I trust the minister will investigate and see if it is not possible to arrange that dredging be done there this summer and that the approaches be put in first-class order so that the wharf can be used. This is a thickly settled district;

there is no railway within 12 or 14 miles, and in the summer time there are 2,000 people in the town of Grand Bend.
_ Mr. PUGSLEY. I quite appreciate the importance of the town of Grand Bend, and it was with a view to accommodating the increasing business that the wharf was built. There was some trouble about the right of way to the approaches and we are taking steps to expropriate. I can tell my hon. friend that there will be as little delay as possible in completing the approaches. In view of the large reduction in the vote for dredging this year I cannot hold out any hope to my hon. friend that dredging will be done at present.
